{"type": "final", "turn": 6, "content": "\n\nCompared both 2025 PA Karst predictions. Here is what came back:\n\n| | KarstBinary | KarstNumber |\n|---|---|---|\n| property | `sample_karst_score` ([0, 1]) | `sample_number` ([0.2, 1.2]) |\n| result_id | 419c581d\u2026f26b | afb1cf68\u20269ae8 |\n| period | 2025 full year | 2025 full year |\n\n**How they differ:** the two maps are different quantities per pixel, so the comparison returns only a correlation \u2014 **\u22120.0172 across 25 compared cells** (36-cell grid; 11 dropped as no-data in either map). That is essentially zero: the two models do not rise and fall together at the same locations. Difference/RMSE/agreement statistics were deliberately withheld because they would mix two different units.\n\n**Which is right:** neither verdict is available here \u2014 these are model-vs-model agreement statistics with no ground truth, and per the tool's warning I can't say one reads higher or lower in a meaningful sense. To answer \"which is right\" you'd need labels: I can run `olmoearth_plan_label_sample` on one (or both) results to design where to label, then estimate each map's error rate. Want me to set that up?", "forced_by_turn_cap": false, "grounding_revised": false, "seq": 14, "t": 163.526, "seconds": 7.353}
